1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,200
 So in order to choose a model,

2
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,920
 sometimes you may want to evaluate that model.

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:09,800
 And you may want to bring some level of rigor when you evaluate that model.

4
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,840
 So you can do on Amazon Bedrock was called automatic evaluation.

5
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:16,640
 So this is to evaluate a model for quality control,

6
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:18,800
 and then you're going to give it some tasks.

7
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:21,200
 So you have some built-in task types.

8
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:26,040
 So just for example, exercises on text summarization, question and answer,

9
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:30,480
 text classification, or open and did text generation.

10
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:32,760
 And so you're going to choose one of these task types,

11
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:35,840
 and then you need to add a prompt data sets.

12
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:42,680
 Or you can use one of the built-in curated prompt data sets from AWS on Amazon Bedrock.

13
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:47,240
 And then thanks to all this, scores are going to be calculated automatically.

14
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:51,000
 So let me show you what I mean in a diagram so you really understand what happens.

15
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,840
 So we have benchmark questions.

16
00:00:52,840 --> 00:00:58,720
 And again, you can bring your own benchmark questions, or you can use the one from AWS.

17
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:02,560
 And then of course, you have questions, but because you've created benchmark,

18
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:06,040
 you need to have benchmark questions as well as benchmark answers.

19
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:13,760
 And the benchmark answers are what would be for you an ideal answer to your benchmark question.

20
00:01:13,760 --> 00:01:16,560
 Then you have the model to evaluate.

21
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:22,720
 And you're going to submit all the benchmark questions into the model that must be evaluated,

22
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:25,480
 which is going to of course generate some answers.

23
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:29,680
 And these answers are generated by a gen AI model.

24
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:35,840
 And then of course, we need to compare the benchmark answers to your generated answers.

25
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:40,480
 So we compare these two, and because we are in an automatic evaluation,

26
00:01:40,480 --> 00:01:45,400
 then it's going to be another model, another gen AI model called a judge model,

27
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:50,960
 which is going to look at the benchmark answer and the generated answer.

28
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:58,440
 And it's going to be asked something along the line of, can you tell if these answers are similar or not?

29
00:01:58,440 --> 00:02:01,160
 And then it's going to give a grading score.

30
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:03,920
 And there are different ways to calculate this grading score.

31
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:10,560
 For example, the bird score or the F1 or so on, but no need to linger on that specific jargon for now.

32
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:13,360
 So a quick note on benchmark datasets.

33
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:14,480
 So they're very helpful.

34
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:19,840
 And a benchmark dataset is a curated collection of data designed specifically to evaluate

35
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,080
 the performance of a language model.

36
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:27,560
 And it can cover many different topics or complexity or even linguistic phenomena.

37
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:29,720
 So why do you use benchmark datasets?

38
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:33,480
 Well, they're very helpful because you can measure the accuracy of your model,

39
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:36,440
 the speed and efficiency and the scalability of your model,

40
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:40,440
 because you may throw a lot of requests at it at the same time.

41
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:46,720
 So some benchmark datasets are designed to allow you to quickly detect any kind of bias

42
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:50,640
 and potential discrimination against a group of people that your model may make.

43
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:52,720
 And this is something the exam can ask you.

44
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:58,800
 And so therefore, using a benchmark dataset gives you a very quick low administrative effort

45
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:01,840
 to evaluate your models for potential bias.

46
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:07,840
 Of course, it is possible for you to also create your own benchmark datasets that are going to be specific

47
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:12,200
 to your business if you need to have specific business criteria.

48
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:14,120
 Of course, we can do also human evaluations.

49
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:19,000
 So this is the exact same idea. We have benchmark questions and benchmark answers,

50
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,960
 but then some humans employees, for example, from the work team could be employees of your company

51
00:03:24,960 --> 00:03:30,000
 or it could be subject matter experts or SME or whatever, are going to look at the benchmark

52
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,360
 answers and the generated answers.

53
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:36,920
 And they're going to say, OK, this looks like correct or not correct.

54
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:38,840
 So how can they evaluate?

55
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:45,040
 Well, there's different type of metrics, there's thumbs up or thumbs down, there's ranking

56
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:49,080
 and so on and then it's going to give a grading score again.

57
00:03:49,080 --> 00:03:53,520
 So this time there's a human part in it and you may prefer it.

58
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:59,840
 You can again choose from the built in test types or you can create a custom task because now humans are evaluating it.

59
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:02,320
 So you are a little more free.

60
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:08,800
 So there are a few metrics you can use to evaluate the output of an FM from a generic perspective.

61
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:12,960
 We have the Rouge, the blur, the birth score and perplexity.

62
00:04:12,960 --> 00:04:17,760
 And I'm going to give you a high level of use to get you understand them and this should be more than enough for the exam.

63
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:22,680
 So Rouge is called recall oriented under study for gifting evaluation.

64
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:29,000
 So here the purpose of it and I think that's what you need to understand from the exam perspective is to evaluate

65
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,640
 automatic summarization and machine translation system.

66
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:37,400
 So very dedicated to these two things and we have a different kind of metrics.

67
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:46,840
 We have Rouge N and N can change between one, two, three, four usually used to measure the number of matching and grams between reference and generated text.

68
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:47,720
 So what does that mean?

69
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:49,640
 That means you have a reference tag.

70
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:57,520
 This is what you would like the output to be of your foundation model and then whatever text has been generated by the foundation model.

71
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:00,680
 And Rouge is going to look at how many and grams are matching.

72
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:06,080
 So if you take a one gram, that means how many words are matching because a one gram is just a word.

73
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:10,440
 But if you take two grams, that means that it's a combination of two words.

74
00:05:10,440 --> 00:05:19,960
 So if you have the apple fail from the tree, you're going to look at the apple, apple fail, fail from the and the tree.

75
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:24,320
 And again, you look at how many matches between your reference text and your generated text.

76
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:27,200
 You should take a very high gram, for example, 10 grams.

77
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:33,600
 You have 10 words matching exactly in the same order from one reference to the generated text.

78
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:37,920
 But it's a very easy one to compute and very easy one to make sense of.

79
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:44,280
 And you have Rouge L, which is going to compute the longest common sub sequence between reference and generate text.

80
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:48,200
 What is the longest sequence of words that is shared between the two texts?

81
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:52,160
 Which makes a lot of sense, for example, you have machine translation systems.

82
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:57,800
 Then you have so Rouge, by the way, is read in French and blue is blue in French, so I just have some colors.

83
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:06,360
 Blue is bilingual evaluation under study. So here, this is to evaluate the quality of generated text, especially for translation.

84
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:15,040
 So this is very for translations and is considered both precision and is going to be as well for too much brevity.

85
00:06:15,040 --> 00:06:18,400
 So it's going to look at a combination of engrams, the formula is a little bit different.

86
00:06:18,400 --> 00:06:22,720
 But if the translation is too short, for example, is going to give a bad score.

87
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:28,640
 So it's a slightly more advanced metric, and I'm not going to do the mechanism under it because you're going to know it.

88
00:06:28,640 --> 00:06:32,400
 But it's very helpful for translations and you to remember it.

89
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:37,520
 But these two things, Rouge and blue, they just look at words, combination of words, and they look at the comparison.

90
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:41,680
 But we have something a bit more advanced, not because of AI, we have the bird's score.

91
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:45,840
 So here we look for the semantic similarity between generated text.

92
00:06:45,840 --> 00:06:52,520
 What does that mean? That means that you're going to compare the actual meaning of the text and see if the meanings are very similar.

93
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:54,240
 So how do we do meaning?

94
00:06:54,240 --> 00:07:02,520
 Well, you're going to have a model and it's going to compare the embeddings of both the text and it can compute the cosine similarity between them.

95
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:08,520
 So embeddings are something we'll see very, very soon and their way to look at a bunch of numbers that represent the text.

96
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:15,520
 And if these numbers are very close between two embeddings, then that means the text are going to be semantically similar.

97
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:20,920
 And so here with the bird's core, we're not looking at individual words, we're looking at the context and the nuance between the text.

98
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:24,200
 So it's a very good one now because we have access to AI.

99
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:28,600
 And perplexity is how well the model will predict the next token.

100
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:29,800
 So lower is better.

101
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:36,440
 And that means that if a model is very confident about the next token, that means that it will be less perplexing therefore more accurate.

102
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:41,720
 So just to give you a diagram, here we have a generative AI model that we train on collection data,

103
00:07:41,720 --> 00:07:44,280
 card data, purchase items, and customer feedback.

104
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:46,840
 And we're going to generate dynamic product descriptions.

105
00:07:46,840 --> 00:07:53,080
 And so from this, we can use the reference one versus the one generated to compute the rouge or the blue metric,

106
00:07:53,080 --> 00:07:57,320
 as well as also look at some similarity in terms of nuance with the bird's core.

107
00:07:57,320 --> 00:08:08,040
 And all of these things can be incorporated back into a feedback loop to make sure we can return the model and get better outputs based on the quality of the scores of these metrics.

108
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:15,640
 On top of just having these type of grading of a foundation model, you may have business metrics to evaluate a model on.

109
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:20,520
 And these are a little bit more difficult to evaluate, of course, but it could be user satisfaction.

110
00:08:20,520 --> 00:08:25,400
 So you gather user feedback and you assess the satisfaction within the model response.

111
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:32,280
 So for example, the user satisfaction of an e-commerce platform or you can compute what is the average revenue per user.

112
00:08:32,280 --> 00:08:40,840
 And of course, well, if the Gen AI app is successful, you hope that this metric will go up or cross the main performance.

113
00:08:40,840 --> 00:08:48,280
 So is the model able to perform across varied task across different domains conversion rights?

114
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:51,960
 So what is the outcome I want? Do I want to have higher conversion rights?

115
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:57,240
 Again, I would monitor this and evaluate my model on that or efficiency.

116
00:08:57,240 --> 00:09:02,840
 What is the efficiency of the model? How much does it cost me? Is it efficient in computation in resource utilization?

117
00:09:02,840 --> 00:09:08,280
 And so on. So that's it for evaluating a foundation model. I hope you like it.

